# Target C: common functional-basis filter for the remaining `k=6, D=-1` Profile B

Date: 2026-09-22

Status: proved a stronger necessary condition and implemented it in the exact Profile B finite cover. No closure is claimed until the full cover executes.

Companion verifier:

`research/verify_target_c_support7_global_segment_relative_six_noroot_k4_d3_profile_b_common_functional_basis_2026_09_22.py`

## 1. Input

The surviving six-row Profile B state has a rank-two coefficient plane, virtual deficiency three, and replacement-functional quotient dimension

```text
s_6 = 6 - 2 + 3 = 7.
```

Its actual singleton replacement classes span this seven-dimensional quotient. Every one-coordinate puncture keeps coefficient rank two and has deficiency `d_i in {2,3}`, hence quotient dimension

```text
s_{5,i} = 3 + d_i in {5,6}.
```

The already committed audit checked that the span of *all* compatible singleton classes fills each of these quotients. It also required a common seven-block set obeying the puncture-kernel loss bounds. That block-only test did not yet require the seven classes on those same blocks to be independent.

## 2. Singleton uniqueness removes state-choice ambiguity

On a virtual-canonical complete block the three singleton constraints are, up to nonzero scalar, the local rows

```text
y, x, x+y.
```

If two singleton states were compatible with the same coefficient space, two of these rows would lie in that space and therefore both `x` and `y` would lie in it. That is exactly the canonical-empty condition, contradicting virtual-canonical completeness.

Therefore every compatible complete block has exactly one singleton state. A seven-block basis candidate has one well-defined replacement-functional class per block; there is no hidden state matching problem.

## 3. Common functional-basis lemma

Let `V_6` be the seven-dimensional six-row replacement-functional quotient. The actual singleton classes span `V_6`, so seven classes on seven distinct actual singleton blocks form a basis `B`.

For puncture `i`, functoriality gives a surjection

```text
pi_i : V_6 -> V_{5,i}.
```

Because `B` is a basis of `V_6`, its image under every `pi_i` spans `V_{5,i}`. A basis block that ceases to be virtual-canonical complete maps to zero. A basis block that remains complete has the same unique compatible singleton state after puncturing, and its class is exactly the corresponding punctured replacement-functional class.

Hence every genuine Profile B state has one common seven-block set such that

1. its six-row singleton classes are independent and have rank seven modulo the six-row canonical relation span; and
2. for every one-coordinate puncture `i`, the surviving classes from those same seven blocks span the full punctured quotient, of rank `3+d_i`.

This strictly strengthens the earlier block-count condition. The count inequalities

```text
d_i=2 => at most two basis blocks disappear,
d_i=3 => at most one basis block disappears
```

remain necessary, but they do not by themselves force the surviving classes to have the required functional rank.

## 4. Exact implementation

The new verifier wraps the coverage-correct Profile B audit without changing its orbit or coefficient-space cover. It first applies the old block-count filter. For every surviving seven-block candidate it then reconstructs the six-row canonical relation columns and the unique singleton functional on each candidate block.

It requires exact rank seven for those seven classes modulo the six-row canonical relation span. It then reconstructs each of the six five-row punctures and requires the images of the same seven-block basis to have exact extra rank `3+d_i`.

These new rank decisions use Python `Fraction` elimination over `Q`; they are not modular tests. The pre-existing modular relation and all-compatible-span tests retain their existing `2^127-1` Hadamard certificate.

The workflow `.github/workflows/target-c-k6-dminus1-profile-b-rank2.yml` now runs this strengthened verifier over the same 32 disjoint orbit shards.

## 5. Execution status

The strengthened workflow attempt triggered by the implementation commit failed before runner execution. The returned jobs have `steps=null`, so none of the mathematical code ran. This is an infrastructure failure and supplies no finite-census result.

The remaining computational bottleneck is therefore unchanged in coverage but stronger in endpoint: execute the full 20,451-orbit Profile B cover with the common functional-basis condition. A zero-survivor result would exclude Profile B under a strictly tighter necessary-condition audit than the previous version. Any survivor must still be lifted through the remaining global nine-block consistency conditions before it can represent the eight-row profile.
